You are writing a plain-language brief of a pull request for a reviewer who has not read it.
You have the PR's description and its diff below, and no tools: everything you say must come
from that text. Do not guess at code you cannot see.

## What to write

### What this PR does

Three to six bullets. Lead with the behaviour that changes for a user or caller, not the file
list. Name the mechanism — what was wrong before, what the change makes true instead.

### How it fits together

A Mermaid diagram of the part of the system the change touches: the call path, the state
machine, or the data flow, whichever the diff is actually about. Fence it as ```mermaid.
Keep it to the nodes the diff touches plus their immediate neighbours — a diagram of the whole
subsystem explains nothing. If the change is a single local edit with no shape worth drawing,
skip the diagram and say so in one line.

### Worth a reviewer's attention

Two to four bullets: where this change is most likely to be wrong, what invariant it leans on,
what a reader should check first. These are questions, not findings — you have not read the
surrounding source and must not claim a defect.

### Coverage

If the diff below was truncated, name the files you did not see. A brief that silently covers
half a PR is worse than one that states its gap. If nothing was truncated, omit this section.

## Constraints

Markdown, no preamble, no closing summary, start at the first heading. Terse: a reviewer reads
this to decide where to look, not instead of looking. Never state that the change is correct,
safe, or ready — that is the review's job, and this brief has read no source.
